 of bowel ischemia markers (no blood in stool, no lactate elevation), and stable vitals (E7/E8) rule out acute ischemic bowel disease as a life-threatening cause of post-exertional abdominal pain?", "result_summary": "The absence of peritoneal signs, ischemia markers, and focal imaging transition points suggests against acute ischemic bowel disease (B3) and mechanical obstruction subtypes (B1.1, B1.2, B1.3), while supporting a paralytic obstruction hypothesis (B2). However, no definitive likelihood ratio evidence was provided for B3, and the overall data pattern indicates significant support for small bowel obstruction (B1.1) via high LR+ for 'untreated medical problems' and other features."}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of peritoneal signs (guarding/rebound), bowel ischemia indicators (e.g., hematochezia, elevated lactate), and documented vascular risk factors in a healthy marathon runner sufficiently rule out acute ischemic bowel disease as a dangerous cause of post-exertional abdominal pain?", "result_summary": "The absence of peritoneal signs, bowel ischemia markers, and vascular risk factors in a healthy marathon runner slightly decreases the likelihood of acute ischemic bowel disease while modestly supporting paralytic obstruction subtypes."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.35860034913494954, "B2": 0.2584722392529635, "B3": 0.016348304390978546, "B1.1": 0.3492430014405268, "B1.2": 0.006766419886719916, "B1.3": 0.0025909278077027877, "B2.1": 0.12527056448691737, "B2.2": 0.13320167476604608}, {"B1": 0.5589723510712367, "B2": 0.07911868613714163, "B3": 0.00934070529246984, "B1.1": 0.5575052857374752, "B1.2": 0.0012816020393277327, "B1.3": 0.0001854632944337984, "B2.1": 0.025783500202984762, "B2.2": 0.05333518593415686}, {"B1": 0.0002710318929546147, "B2": 0.08572335514571885, "B3": 0.003511716739767594, "B1.1": 0.5